<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Cove runs outside the App Sandbox.

	  It was inside it until the screenshot watcher, which is what forced the
	  question: a sandboxed app cannot read `com.apple.screencapture`'s own
	  `location` preference — the domain resolves to the container and comes back
	  empty — so it cannot know where screenshots go. The only way in was to make
	  the user point at the folder through an open panel and keep the grant as a
	  bookmark, which asks a question Cove already had the means to answer, and
	  quietly breaks the day they move the folder.

	  The sandbox bought nothing else here. Cove is distributed through GitHub
	  releases rather than the App Store, so nothing required it, and everything
	  it does is still local: the one network call is link previews, behind its
	  own switch. Access to Desktop and Documents is now governed by TCC, which
	  asks once, in the system's own words, and can be revoked in System Settings
	  — a better contract than a folder grant buried in Cove's own preferences.

	  The widget extension keeps its sandbox: WidgetKit requires it, and the app
	  group below is what the two of them share the store through.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.loop.cove</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
